1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,250
Two shops on the same street.

2
00:00:02,450 --> 00:00:04,883
Shop A is selling for ten lakh rupees.

3
00:00:05,083 --> 00:00:07,259
Shop B is selling for fifty lakh.

4
00:00:07,459 --> 00:00:08,789
Which one is cheaper?

5
00:00:08,989 --> 00:00:10,122
Trick question.

6
00:00:10,322 --> 00:00:12,287
You cannot know from the price alone.

7
00:00:13,037 --> 00:00:15,717
Because what you are really buying is not the shop.

8
00:00:15,917 --> 00:00:18,371
It is the profit the shop makes every year.

9
00:00:18,571 --> 00:00:20,364
Shop A earns one lakh a year.

10
00:00:20,564 --> 00:00:22,459
Shop B earns ten lakh a year.

11
00:00:23,209 --> 00:00:25,663
So Shop A takes ten years to pay you back.

12
00:00:25,863 --> 00:00:27,592
Shop B takes just five.

13
00:00:27,792 --> 00:00:30,992
The expensive-looking shop is actually the cheaper deal.

14
00:00:31,742 --> 00:00:33,939
That is the whole game of valuation.

15
00:00:34,139 --> 00:00:35,668
Price is what you pay.

16
00:00:35,868 --> 00:00:37,453
Value is what you get.

17
00:00:37,653 --> 00:00:40,741
A valuation ratio simply puts the two side by side,

18
00:00:40,741 --> 00:00:43,797
so you can compare any business with any other.

19
00:00:44,547 --> 00:00:46,761
A share works exactly like that shop.

20
00:00:46,961 --> 00:00:49,641
And every number you need, profit, assets, debt,

21
00:00:49,641 --> 00:00:52,359
is already sitting in the company's accounts.

22
00:00:52,559 --> 00:00:55,775
The next three lessons show where to find them,

23
00:00:55,775 --> 00:00:59,621
and what to do with them: P/E, P/B, and EV to EBITDA.

